14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 6, 1862, about 4 p.m. to 5:30 p.m.; W.H.L. Wallace died April 10","place":"Duncan Field, the Hornet’s Nest, the Corinth Road, and the final United States line above Pittsburg Landing, Tennessee","result":"Patton Anderson’s frontal attack failed. Daniel Ruggles then assembled 11 Confederate batteries with as many as 53 guns and opened a sustained bombardment. At about 4 p.m., Sherman and McClernand withdrew from the west while Hurlbut withdrew from the east, leaving W.H.L. Wallace and Benjamin Prentiss exposed on both flanks. Wallace ordered a retreat but was shot in the head while directing it. Several regiments escaped; most of the 58th Illinois, 12th Iowa, 14th Iowa, and Prentiss’s remaining command were surrounded. Prentiss and about 2,200 United States soldiers surrendered at about 5:30 p.m. Wallace was recovered alive the next day and died on April 10","facts":[{"label":"Anderson’s attack","value":"Four regiments and two battalions; repulsed by close-range fire"},{"label":"Ruggles’s artillery","value":"11 batteries and as many as 53 guns"},{"label":"Bombardment began","value":"About 4 p.m."},{"label":"United States flanks","value":"Sherman and McClernand withdrew on the right; Hurlbut withdrew on the left"},{"label":"Wallace’s withdrawal","value":"Ordered after Thomas Sweeney reported that his remaining two regiments could not hold"},{"label":"Wallace wounded","value":"Shot through the head while directing Tuttle’s brigade toward the Corinth Road"},{"label":"Captured","value":"About 2,200 men from the 2nd and 6th Divisions, including Benjamin Prentiss"},{"label":"Position surrendered","value":"About 5:30 p.m."}],"maps":[{"title":"Shiloh battlefield today","caption":"The NPS map locates Duncan Field, the Hornet’s Nest, the Corinth Road, the Hamburg–Savannah Road, and Pittsburg Landing.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"The center before the final attacks","caption":"Wallace and Prentiss held the farm lane north of Duncan Field while the United States left and right still protected their flanks.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/hornets-nest-tour-stop-10.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-hornets-nest-noon.svg","alt":"Map of the United States center and earlier Confederate attacks at Shiloh"},{"title":"Collapse of the Hornet’s Nest","caption":"Ruggles’s artillery bombarded from the south. Withdrawals by Sherman, McClernand, and Hurlbut opened both flanks; Confederates then surrounded Wallace’s and Prentiss’s remaining troops.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/ruggles-battery-tour-stop-4.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-hornets-nest-collapse.svg","alt":"Map of the artillery bombardment and encirclement of the Hornet’s Nest at Shiloh"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"This 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the roads, fields, camps, and army positions used to understand the late-afternoon encirclement.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of the Battle of Shiloh"}],"moments":[{"time":"5:47","audioTime":347,"title":"The attack shifted to the United States center","detail":"Patton Anderson took four regiments and two battalions across Duncan Field against W.H.L. Wallace’s line."},{"time":"6:37","audioTime":397,"title":"Anderson’s attack was repulsed","detail":"Wallace’s infantry waited in the thickets and along the farm lane, then stopped the advance with close-range volleys."},{"time":"7:04","audioTime":424,"title":"Beauregard assigned Ruggles to the center","detail":"Daniel Ruggles took responsibility for the Confederate attacks against Wallace and Prentiss."},{"time":"8:27","audioTime":507,"title":"Ruggles decided to mass artillery","detail":"After earlier frontal attacks failed, he sent his staff to collect batteries from across the battlefield."},{"time":"9:02","audioTime":542,"title":"The Confederate batteries assembled","detail":"The podcast describes all or parts of 12 batteries and about 60 guns; the NPS identifies 11 batteries and as many as 53 guns."},{"time":"9:25","audioTime":565,"title":"The bombardment began","detail":"The guns opened at about 4 p.m. and continued arriving until about 5 p.m. Many rounds passed over infantry sheltering close to the ground, but some defenders were killed or wounded."},{"time":"11:49","audioTime":709,"title":"Sherman and McClernand withdrew","detail":"At about 4 p.m., the two divisions moved northeast and formed along the Hamburg–Savannah Road, roughly one mile from the river."},{"time":"12:25","audioTime":745,"title":"Hurlbut withdrew from the United States left","detail":"Confederates moved between his flank and the river. His attempt to form at Cloud Field failed, and parts of the division ran to the final ridge above Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"14:03","audioTime":843,"title":"Hurlbut rallied troops around the siege guns","detail":"Units that retained organization became rally points, and field batteries were placed beside the 24-pounder siege guns."},{"time":"14:59","audioTime":899,"title":"Prentiss’s left flank became exposed","detail":"Hurlbut’s courier was killed before delivering notice of the withdrawal. Prentiss bent his line back but did not immediately retreat."},{"time":"16:11","audioTime":971,"title":"Grant’s earlier order remained in effect","detail":"Grant had told Wallace and Prentiss to hold. Wallace now had to decide when continued resistance would result in encirclement."},{"time":"17:33","audioTime":1053,"title":"Sweeney reported that the right could not hold","detail":"Only the 7th and 58th Illinois remained with his brigade; Confederates were already moving around them."},{"time":"19:06","audioTime":1146,"title":"Wallace ordered a withdrawal","detail":"Artillery left first. The 7th Illinois and the 2nd and 7th Iowa moved toward the Corinth Road as Confederate forces closed in."},{"time":"19:42","audioTime":1182,"title":"W.H.L. Wallace was mortally wounded","detail":"A bullet entered the back of his head and exited near the left eye. His aides left him beside the road when Confederate fire made it impossible to carry him farther."},{"time":"20:39","audioTime":1239,"title":"Several regiments were trapped","detail":"Most of the 58th Illinois and the 12th and 14th Iowa were cut off north of the farm lane. Most of Prentiss’s remaining command was also surrounded."},{"time":"21:54","audioTime":1314,"title":"The Hornet’s Nest surrendered","detail":"Confederates captured Prentiss and about 2,200 men from the 2nd and 6th Divisions at about 5:30 p.m."},{"time":"24:12","audioTime":1452,"title":"Wallace was recovered alive","detail":"On April 7, Cyrus Dickey found Wallace still breathing. He was taken to Cherry Mansion in Savannah, where he died four days after being wounded."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Ordered the center to hold and organized the final line above Pittsburg Landing"},{"commander":"W.H.L. Wallace","command":"2nd Division","subordinates":"Tuttle’s and Sweeney’s brigades; ordered the late withdrawal and was mortally wounded"},{"commander":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","command":"6th Division remnant","subordinates":"Held the left half of the Hornet’s Nest and surrendered with most of his remaining command"},{"commander":"Thomas W. Sweeney","command":"Brigade, Wallace’s 2nd Division","subordinates":"7th and 58th Illinois remained after four regiments had been detached"},{"commander":"James M. Tuttle","command":"Brigade, Wallace’s 2nd Division","subordinates":"2nd, 7th, 12th, and 14th Iowa during the withdrawal"},{"commander":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Withdrew from the east and rallied survivors around the siege guns"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman / John A. McClernand","command":"United States right","subordinates":"Withdrew northeast to the Hamburg–Savannah Road at about 4 p.m."}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Assigned Ruggles to direct operations against the United States center"},{"commander":"Daniel Ruggles","command":"Confederate attack on the center","subordinates":"Assembled the massed artillery and coordinated pressure on the Hornet’s Nest"},{"commander":"Patton Anderson","command":"Brigade, Ruggles’s Division","subordinates":"Led the failed attack across Duncan Field before the artillery concentration"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg and other field commanders","command":"Infantry converging on the center","subordinates":"Confederate brigades moved around both exposed flanks and closed the escape routes"}]}],"people":[{"name":"W.H.L. Wallace","role":"Commanded the 2nd Division, ordered its withdrawal, and was mortally wounded while directing it","side":"United States"},{"name":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","role":"Held the left half of the Hornet’s Nest after Hurlbut withdrew and surrendered with most of his command","side":"United States","image":"/images/benjamin-prentiss.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666477/"},{"name":"Daniel Ruggles","role":"Assembled the Confederate artillery concentration and directed operations against the center","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"P.G.T. Beauregard","role":"Assigned Ruggles to the Hornet’s Nest after taking command of the Confederate army","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas W. Sweeney","role":"Reported that Wallace’s exposed right flank could no longer hold after the neighboring division withdrew","side":"United States"},{"name":"James M. Tuttle","role":"Commanded the Iowa brigade that attempted to withdraw north from the Hornet’s Nest","side":"United States"},{"name":"Stephen A. Hurlbut","role":"Withdrew from the left and rebuilt part of his division at Grant’s final line","side":"United States"},{"name":"Patton Anderson","role":"Led the unsuccessful late-afternoon infantry attack across Duncan Field","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Cyrus Dickey","role":"Wallace’s aide and brother-in-law; tried to carry him from the field and recovered him alive on April 7","side":"United States"},{"name":"Ann Dickey Wallace","role":"Treated wounded soldiers aboard a steamboat and cared for her husband after his recovery from the field","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Brigadier General W.H.L. Wallace","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 2nd Division, Army of the Tennessee","outcome":"Shot through the head while directing the withdrawal; recovered alive April 7 and died at Savannah on April 10, 1862"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/benjamin-prentiss.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Benjamin M. Prentiss","caption":"Prentiss remained in the Hornet’s Nest after both flanks withdrew and surrendered with most of his command.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666477/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"The woods, thickets, and shallow farm road provided cover but made an organized withdrawal difficult.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing in 1862","caption":"Hurlbut’s survivors rallied around artillery on the ridge above Pittsburg Landing while wounded soldiers crowded the river transports.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 121","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/121-shiloh-part-the-eleventh"},{"label":"National Park Service: Hornet’s Nest","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/hornets-nest-tour-stop-10.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Ruggles’ Battery","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/ruggles-battery-tour-stop-4.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Prentiss’s 6th Division tablet","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=I"},{"label":"National Park Service: Shiloh maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Benjamin Prentiss portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018666477/"}]}}]}]
